You're asking pc, but here are my answers:

What is the relationship between {piso'iva'e} and {mutce}, if any? 
In an ontology (is there a better term?) without thresholds between
True and False, they mean the same thing.
In an ontology with thresholds between True and False, they mean 
different things.
We don't want ambiguity, so the question must be settled one way or
the other.

Does {so'iva'e} make any sense?
In an ontology without thresholds between True and False, it makes 
no sense, unless it's merely an abbreviation for {pi so'i va'e}.
In an ontology without thresholds between True and False, it makes 
sense, and would mean "very".

